WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.800
What's up? Welcome to Models and Agents for Beginners. Episode 72. It's June 15th,

00:00:05.800 --> 00:00:12.160
2026. Let's break down today's coolest AI news so anyone can understand it. Let's go.

00:00:12.920 --> 00:00:18.120
Picture this. One AI model can quietly pass along strange little habits to the next one

00:00:18.120 --> 00:00:23.540
during training. Those habits might show up as odd word choices or even bigger issues

00:00:23.540 --> 00:00:29.820
like overly negative tones. The process is called distillation. It works when a newer or

00:00:29.820 --> 00:00:34.740
smaller model studies the answers from a bigger model instead of learning everything from

00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:40.400
raw data all over again. Think of it like a student teacher who picked up a quirky problem

00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:45.340
solving trick from their own mentor. The school might try to correct it but the style still

00:00:45.340 --> 00:00:51.000
lingers. Another way to picture it is passing a recipe down through generations. Each

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:55.940
cook adds their own tiny twist without realizing it and soon the dish tastes different from

00:00:55.940 --> 00:01:01.060
the original. This matters because it helps explain why models from the same company often

00:01:01.060 --> 00:01:07.380
feel similar in personality even when developers try to make them unique. For anyone using AI

00:01:07.380 --> 00:01:12.660
in school projects or creative hobbies it means the tools you rely on might carry forward

00:01:12.660 --> 00:01:18.500
hidden quirks from earlier versions. Those quirks can affect how consistent or reliable the

00:01:18.500 --> 00:01:24.260
answers feel over time. Safety research becomes even more important as these patterns get

00:01:24.260 --> 00:01:30.120
passed along without anyone noticing right away. Right now there is no direct demo for this

00:01:30.120 --> 00:01:35.220
finding but you can compare how different models respond by trying the exact same prompt

00:01:35.220 --> 00:01:42.880
in free versions of chat GPT, Claude or Gemini and noticing the tone shifts. Okay now from

00:01:42.880 --> 00:01:47.820
my favorite part of the show the deep dive where we look under the hood. You know how when

00:01:47.820 --> 00:01:52.020
you are texting a friend your phone suggests the next word based on what you have typed

00:01:52.020 --> 00:01:58.680
so far. Sometimes it even starts copying your own texting style without you noticing. Now imagine

00:01:58.680 --> 00:02:04.160
a whole group of phones learning from each other. The newer phones study the messages the older phones

00:02:04.160 --> 00:02:11.400
send and try to copy those patterns exactly. Step by step the new phones get faster at predicting words

00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:18.220
but they also start using the same odd phrases or shortcuts the old phones liked. Even if someone tries

00:02:18.220 --> 00:02:23.660
to clean up the new phones by removing the weird phrases later it does not always work because the

00:02:23.660 --> 00:02:28.980
habit is baked in to how the phones learned the patterns in the first place. And that is basically

00:02:28.980 --> 00:02:35.900
what happens during distillation when one AI model trains the next one. The new model absorbs not just

00:02:35.900 --> 00:02:42.720
the useful knowledge but also the strange little behaviors. So next time someone says model distillation

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:48.600
you can tell them it is basically phones copying each other's texting quirks. Not so scary right?

00:02:49.300 --> 00:02:55.060
Let's move to some cool stuff you can actually try yourself. When someone who already knows film or

00:02:55.060 --> 00:03:00.740
photography uses AI they are not just hitting a button for quick results. They treat it like a practice

00:03:00.740 --> 00:03:06.520
partner that helps them experiment with new ideas and then bring those lessons back to their real work.

00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:12.380
It is like having a sketchbook that instantly shows you variations of an idea so you can decide which

00:03:12.380 --> 00:03:18.060
direction feels right. Friends in the film industry are now finishing more short projects on their

00:03:18.060 --> 00:03:23.580
own than they did in years of traditional jobs and the same pattern appears in photography and

00:03:23.580 --> 00:03:30.460
storytelling. If you like making videos or editing photos for fun or school projects this means AI

00:03:30.460 --> 00:03:37.020
can become a creative sparring partner rather than just a shortcut. Open a free image or video tool

00:03:37.020 --> 00:03:43.780
like the built-in editor in CapCut or the image features in chat GPT. Type a specific creative prompt

00:03:43.780 --> 00:03:49.980
such as turn this photo into a moody film still with dramatic lighting. Notice how the output gives

00:03:49.980 --> 00:03:56.100
you ideas you can then tweak yourself. Next up is a new system from Microsoft Research called Marage

00:03:56.100 --> 00:04:02.420
that helps video generating AI remember a scene's layout even when the camera moves around for a long

00:04:02.420 --> 00:04:08.660
time. Instead of forgetting details and creating glitches it stores the space information in a smart

00:04:08.660 --> 00:04:14.960
way that uses less computer power. Think of it like a director who keeps a detailed map of every room

00:04:14.960 --> 00:04:20.640
in a movie set so characters can walk through consistently without the background suddenly changing.

00:04:21.500 --> 00:04:27.020
This could make AI generated videos feel more like real movies where the world stays steady as the

00:04:27.020 --> 00:04:33.780
camera follows someone down a hallway. For anyone who enjoys making short films or animations on

00:04:33.780 --> 00:04:40.380
their phone it points to tools that will soon feel less random and more reliable. Experiment with

00:04:40.380 --> 00:04:46.340
current free video tools like runways free tier or picolabs by typing a prompt that involves a

00:04:46.340 --> 00:04:51.700
moving camera such as a character walking through a hallway while the camera follows from behind.

00:04:52.500 --> 00:04:57.620
Watch how well the background stays steady. Time for quick bits the rapid fire section.

